  "worked_solution": "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $T$ is a function $F(t)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(t)=P(T\\le t).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(T>a)=1-P(T\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we want to compute $P(T\\ge 2).$ However, this interval is stated in the form $T\\ge 2,$ not $T>2.$\n\nSo first, we must convert the interval $T\\ge 2$ to the form $T>a.$ Looking at the given possible values of $T,$ we see that $T\\ge 2$ is equivalent to $T>\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T\\ge 2) & =P(T>\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =1-P(T\\le \\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =1-F(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
